.\" karvi-audit(1): the audit trail reader's manual page. Written by hand
.\" except the two generated regions, which tools/mangen rewrites from the
.\" reader's flag definition (make generate); docs/OPERATIONS.md "The audit
.\" trail" is the guide to the trail itself, so a change to the reader or to
.\" the trail changes both.
.TH KARVI-AUDIT 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and options are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi\-audit \- show karvi's audit trail from journald, one event per row
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SYNOPSIS: tools/mangen from internal/auditview; edit there
.B karvi\-audit
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ "\-g \fIPATTERN\fP" ]
.RB [ "\-o \fINAME\fP" ]...
.RB [ "\-d \fINAME\fP" ]...
.RB [ "\-n \fIN\fP" ]
.RB [ "\-S \fITIME\fP" ]
.RB [ "\-U \fITIME\fP" ]
.RB [ \-\-nocolor ]
.\" END GENERATED SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B karvi\-audit
reads the audit trail, the events every job and every login writes to
journald, and shows each event as one row of a table: what was run, by
whom, at which device, in which job, and how it ended.
It runs
.BR journalctl (1)
with the trail's matches, the identifier
.B karvi
and the audit's
.BR MESSAGE_ID ,
which tell an audit event from the daemon's log under the same
identifier.
.PP
It reads no configuration.
The window
.RB ( \-S ,
.BR \-U ,
.BR \-n ),
the pattern
.RB ( \-g ),
the order
.RB ( \-r ),
and the follow
.RB ( \-f )
are passed to
.B journalctl
as given; the operator
.RB ( \-o )
and the device
.RB ( \-d )
are matched as the event's fields, a field given more than once matching
any of its names, and the operator and the device together matching
both.
Who reads whose events is journald's: an operator reads their own, and
the members of
.B adm
and
.B systemd\-journal
everyone's.
.PP
An event it cannot read, one with no
.BR KARVI_AUDIT_JSON ,
JSON that does not decode, or an audit schema other than 2, has no row;
one line on standard error at the end counts them, a follow ended by
Ctrl\-C included:
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit: 12 events not shown: not audit schema 2
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED OPTIONS: tools/mangen from internal/auditview; edit there
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Show the newest events, then each new one as it comes, until Ctrl\-C (journalctl's \-\-follow).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse
The newest event first (journalctl's \-\-reverse).
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-grep " \fIPATTERN\fP"
The events whose line matches PATTERN, a job's ID or a statement's words among them (journalctl's \-\-grep: a regular expression, its case ignored when it is all lowercase).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-operator " \fINAME\fP"
The events of NAME, as the operator or as the login user behind sudo, matched whole; repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " \fINAME\fP"
The events at the device NAME, lowercased and matched whole; repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-lines " \fIN\fP"
The newest N events (journalctl's \-\-lines); every event when not given, ten under \-\-follow.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-since " \fITIME\fP"
The events at or after TIME (journalctl's \-\-since: 2026\-10\-17 14:00, yesterday, \-1h).
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-until " \fITIME\fP"
The events at or before TIME (journalctl's \-\-until).
.TP
.B \-\-nocolor
No colour, at a terminal or through a pipe.
.\" END GENERATED OPTIONS
.SH OUTPUT
The heading and one row per event on standard output, in the journal's
order:
.PP
.nf
.RS
JOB\-ID                OPERATOR      MODE  EVENT   OUTCOME                      DEVICE             COMMAND
261017\-171016\-00       netops       run   start
261017\-171016\-00       netops       run   command success                    fake\-iosxe           show clock
261017\-171016\-00       netops       run   command connection_error           dead\-iosxe           show clock
261017\-171016\-00       netops       run   end     error
.RE
.fi
.PP
The columns, at fixed widths with one space between them:
.TP
.B JOB\-ID
16 cells: the job's ID, or the login's.
.TP
.B OPERATOR
18 cells: the account the job or the login ran as, a job through the
daemon its client's; or
.IB OPERATOR / LOGINUSER
when the event names a login user, the person behind
.BR sudo ,
the operator's part cut first.
.TP
.B MODE
5 cells: the kind,
.BR cmd ,
.BR run ,
.BR crun ,
or
.BR login .
.TP
.B EVENT
7 cells:
.BR start ,
.B command
(once per device per statement),
.BR cancel ,
.B after
(a collection's hook), or
.BR end .
.TP
.B OUTCOME
16 cells: a
.B command
event's status, an
.BR end 's
final status, a login's
.B complete
or
.BR error ,
or the hook's
.B success
or
.BR fail ;
none for a
.B start
or a
.BR cancel .
.TP
.B DEVICE
30 cells: the device's canonical name.
.TP
.B COMMAND
The statement as sent, masked as the audit masks it; a tab, a newline, or
another control character is written as
.BR \e t ,
.BR \e n ,
or
.BR \e x1b .
.PP
A cell longer than its column is cut with \[u2026].
At a terminal the line is cut with \[u2026] so that its last cell stays free,
COMMAND cut or not shown at all; to a pipe or a file nothing cuts the line.
OPERATOR and DEVICE are centred in their columns while the width holds
the column whole, and at the column's left when it cuts into it.
.PP
The heading is blue, OPERATOR cyan, DEVICE bold yellow, and OUTCOME by
its word: green for
.BR success ,
.BR complete ,
and
.BR exercise ;
orange for work not done or cut short,
.BR cancel ,
.BR halt ,
.BR incomplete ,
and a command's
.BR not_attempted_* ,
.BR not_started_* ,
and
.BR incomplete_* ;
red for every other word.
The colour goes to a terminal and through a pipe, so a pager keeps it
.RB ( "karvi\-audit | less \-R" ),
never into a file;
.B \-\-nocolor
and
.B NO_COLOR
turn it off.
.PP
Not following, the heading comes with the first row, and when
.B journalctl
returned no entry,
.B "karvi\-audit: no matches"
goes to standard error, with no heading.
Following, the heading comes with the first row, or once
.B journalctl
has run half a second without ending.
A reader that quits, such as
.B less
or
.BR head ,
ends it quietly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B journalctl
ended 0, nothing matched included, or a follow was ended by Ctrl\-C or by
its reader's closing.
.TP
.B 1
.B journalctl
is missing
.RB ( "karvi\-audit: journalctl not found" ),
or it ended otherwise; a value it refused, a time, a pattern, or
.B \-r
with
.BR \-f ,
is its message on standard error as it wrote it.
Or the table could not be written, other than to a reader that quit.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown flag, a positional argument, or an
.B \-n
that is not a number.
The message and the help go to standard error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and not empty, no colour, as
.BR \-\-nocolor .
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.B journalctl
is found.
.SH EXAMPLES
A job's events, by its ID:
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit \-g 261017\-171016\-00
.RE
.fi
.PP
Today's events at one device, newest first:
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit \-d core\-nyc\-01 \-S today \-r
.RE
.fi
.PP
The last hour's events of one person, as the operator or behind
.BR sudo :
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit \-o jsmith \-S \-1h
.RE
.fi
.PP
The trail as it is written, through a shift, until Ctrl\-C:
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit \-f
.RE
.fi
.PP
Yesterday's trail, paged with its colours:
.PP
.nf
.RS
karvi\-audit \-S yesterday \-U today | less \-R
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR karvi (1),
.BR karvi\-job (1),
.BR karvi\-prune (8);
.I docs/OPERATIONS.md
"The audit trail" in the karvi source.
